      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-07-03">Published July 3, 2026</time></p>

      <div set:html={`
<p>On February 18, 2027, a European Union regulation goes into effect that will fundamentally change how e-readers are designed, sold, and repaired. The EU Battery Law requires all smartphones, tablets, and e-readers sold in Europe to feature user-replaceable batteries. For the millions of people who read EPUB files on a Kindle, Kobo, PocketBook, or Boox device, this law means longer-lasting devices, easier repairs, and a shift away from planned obsolescence.</p> <p>The regulation is part of a broader EU push for sustainability and the right to repair. Devices must be designed so consumers can easily remove and replace batteries using basic commercially available tools — no heat guns, no adhesive solvents, no specialized training required. For e-reader owners, who often keep their devices for five years or longer, this is a meaningful change.</p> <p>Here is what the EU Battery Law means for your e-reader, which companies are prepared, and how EPUB readers can make the most of this shift.</p> <h2>What the EU Battery Law Requires</h2> <p>The regulation, formally part of the EU's Ecodesign for Sustainable Products Regulation (ESPR), sets three key requirements for e-readers sold in Europe from February 18, 2027 onward:</p> <ul> <li><strong>User-replaceable batteries.</strong> The battery must be removable and replaceable with commercially available tools (screwdriver, spudger, or similar). No permanent adhesives or specialized heating equipment may be required.</li> <li><strong>Spare parts availability.</strong> Manufacturers must sell replacement batteries for at least five years after a device model is discontinued. They must also provide technical documentation and repair instructions.</li> <li><strong>Clear battery health information.</strong> Devices must display battery health metrics so users can decide when a replacement is genuinely needed, rather than being forced to upgrade the whole device.</li> </ul> <p>The law applies to all devices sold in the EU, regardless of where the manufacturer is based. A device sold in Paris or Berlin must comply, even if the same model sold in New York or Tokyo uses a glued-in battery. In practice, most manufacturers are expected to unify their designs worldwide rather than maintain separate EU and non-EU production lines.</p> <h2>Amazon Kindle: Finally Ready for Replaceable Batteries</h2> <p>Amazon, the dominant player in the e-reader market, is preparing for the EU law. Evidence comes from an unlikely source: a firmware leak. In the recently pulled Kindle firmware version 5.19.4, users discovered text strings referencing a battery replacement workflow:</p> <blockquote> <p>"This battery cannot be recognized and may not perform as expected. Charging has been limited to protect your device. To restore your device to its original performance specifications, we recommend installing a battery that meets Amazon specifications. Go to Settings &gt; Device Options &gt; Battery for battery troubleshooting guidance and support. Scan the QR code below to purchase a battery replacement kit and view the replacement instructions."</p> </blockquote> <p>This is a clear signal that Amazon is building battery replacement infrastructure for upcoming Kindle models. The fact that the firmware includes QR-code-based parts ordering and step-by-step instructions suggests Amazon is working on a self-service repair model similar to what Apple and Samsung offer for smartphones.</p> <p>For EPUB readers who use a Kindle — and millions do, even though Kindle is not natively EPUB-friendly — this means future Kindles will last significantly longer. A dead battery has historically been the end of life for an e-reader. With user-replaceable batteries, a device that could otherwise serve for a decade becomes viable again with a $20-$50 battery swap.</p> <h2>Rakuten Kobo: Already Ahead of the Curve</h2> <p>Kobo is in the strongest position. More than a year ago, Rakuten Kobo partnered with iFixit to sell replacement batteries, screen repair kits, and other spare parts for their devices. Current Kobo models — including the Clara BW, Clara Colour, Libra Colour, and Sage — have genuine replacement batteries available through the iFixit store for between $20 and $70, depending on the model.</p> <p>Kobo's partnership with iFixit covers not just batteries but also screens, cases, and repair toolkits, making it the most repair-friendly major e-reader brand on the market. For EPUB readers who prioritize device longevity, this is a significant advantage over sealed-device competitors.</p> <p>Kobo's native EPUB support — the best of any major e-reader — combined with user-replaceable batteries makes it the standout choice for readers who want a device that will last. If you sideload EPUB files using Adobe Digital Editions or Calibre, a Kobo is the device most likely to still be functional a decade from now.</p> <h2>reMarkable: A Paper-Like Approach to Repairability</h2> <p>The reMarkable Paper, released as an update to the reMarkable 2, shifts away from glued chassis to a repair-friendly screw-and-snap design. It houses a 3,820 mAh battery that can be physically swapped when the original cell degrades. However, replacement batteries are not yet available for purchase directly from reMarkable, so this is a work in progress.</p> <p>For EPUB readers who use reMarkable devices — primarily those who read PDFs, EPUBs, and take handwritten notes — the repairable design is welcome news. The device's Linux-based operating system also supports Calibre integration for wireless EPUB transfer, making it a viable option for the privacy-focused EPUB reader.</p> <h2>The Challenger Brands: Boox, PocketBook, Bigme, and Others Face an Uphill Battle</h2> <p>While Amazon and Kobo are prepared, the rest of the e-reader industry is not. Smaller manufacturers including Onyx Boox, PocketBook, Bigme, Meebook, iFlytek, Viwoods, Dasung, and Tolino all sell devices in the EU and are underprepared for the February 2027 deadline.</p> <p>These companies face significant challenges:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Redesign costs.</strong> Moving from a glued-sealed chassis to a snap-and-screw design requires retooling manufacturing lines, sourcing new enclosure components, and re-engineering internal layouts. For companies with thin margins and small production runs, this can cost millions.</li> <li><strong>Parts supply chains.</strong> Establishing a spare-parts pipeline — including battery sourcing, warehousing, distribution, and retail channels — is expensive. Smaller companies lack the scale to partner with iFixit-style distributors.</li> <li><strong>Documentation and support.</strong> Writing repair manuals, training support staff, and setting up QR-code-based ordering systems requires resources that smaller hardware companies may not have.</li> </ul> <p>Some of these companies may choose to exit the EU market entirely, rather than invest in compliance. For EPUB readers in Europe, this could mean a narrower selection of devices from brands like Boox and PocketBook. For readers outside the EU, the global designs may eventually benefit from these changes anyway — manufacturers who retool for Europe often carry those designs worldwide.</p> <p>The EU's ebook market is substantial: 95% of internet users in the EU have bought ebooks or audiobooks, the market is valued at $101.6 billion, and it grows at 7.1% annually. Europe will not be abandoned. The question is which brands survive the compliance transition.</p> <h2>What This Means for EPUB Readers</h2> <p>The EU Battery Law is not just a regulation — it is a structural shift in how e-readers are built and valued. Here is what EPUB readers should consider:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Your next e-reader will last longer.</strong> When the battery degrades after 3-5 years of daily reading, you replace the battery, not the device. This is especially meaningful for EPUB readers, who accumulate libraries over decades and value device continuity.</li> <li><strong>Kobo is the safest bet right now.</strong> Kobo already has replaceable batteries, iFixit partnerships, and native EPUB support. If you are buying an e-reader today and want long-term usability, Kobo is the clear choice.</li> <li><strong>New Kindles may finally support battery swaps.</strong> The firmware leak suggests upcoming Kindle models will include battery replacement infrastructure. If you prefer the Kindle ecosystem, waiting for the next generation may be wise.</li> <li><strong>Smaller brands may disappear from Europe.</strong> If you rely on a niche brand like Boox or Bigme for specific reading workflows, check whether they have announced EU compliance plans. If not, stock up on spare parts while they are available.</li> <li><strong>Your EPUB library is device-independent.</strong> One advantage of EPUB as a format is that it is not tied to any hardware ecosystem. Whether your current e-reader lasts another five years or you need to switch brands, your EPUB files move with you. This is the fundamental argument for DRM-free EPUB libraries and using tools like Calibre to manage your collection.</li> </ul> <h2>Planning Your E-Reader Purchase Around the EU Battery Law</h2> <p>If you are in the market for a new e-reader and the EU Battery Law matters to you, here is a purchasing timeline:</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Timeline</th> <th>Recommendation</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><strong>Now (July 2026)</strong></td> <td>Kobo devices are the only major e-readers with confirmed replaceable batteries and iFixit parts availability. If you buy today, get a Kobo.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Late 2026</strong></td> <td>Amazon is expected to release next-generation Kindles with battery replacement support. If you prefer Kindle, wait for these models.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Early 2027</strong></td> <td>The February 18 deadline forces compliance. All e-readers sold in the EU must have replaceable batteries. Prices may rise as manufacturers absorb redesign costs.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Late 2027+</strong></td> <td>The market stabilizes. E-readers last longer, repair options are mainstream, and the secondary market for used devices becomes more viable.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>One thing that will not change: EPUB remains the universal format for ebooks, and <a href="/">converter-epub.com</a> continues to support every major format conversion free of charge, online, with no data upload required.</p> <h2>How to Protect Your EPUB Library During the Transition</h2> <p>Regardless of which e-reader brand you choose, the most important step is to keep your EPUB library in a device-independent format:</p> <ul> <li>Store your EPUB files in a <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre library</a> with full metadata. Calibre runs on Windows, macOS, and Linux and automatically organizes your books by author, title, series, and tags.</li> <li>Remove DRM from your purchased ebooks where legally permitted, so they remain readable if you switch devices. The EU Battery Law does not address DRM, but your right to repair your device is meaningless if you cannot read your books on it.</li> <li>Back up your library to an external drive or cloud storage. A Calibre library is just a folder of EPUB files — it can be copied and restored on any computer.</li> <li>Use <a href="/">converter-epub.com</a> to convert any non-EPUB files (PDF, DOCX, MOBI, AZW3) to EPUB, ensuring your entire collection works on any e-reader, regardless of brand.</li> </ul> <p>The EU Battery Law is a rare piece of legislation that directly benefits consumers and the environment. For EPUB readers, it means e-readers that last longer, require less e-waste, and give you more freedom to choose your reading device based on features — not based on fear of battery death.</p> <section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage"> <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">When does the EU Battery Law take effect for e-readers?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>February 18, 2027. After this date, all e-readers, smartphones, and tablets sold in the European Union must have user-replaceable batteries.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Does the EU Battery Law apply to e-readers sold outside Europe?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>The law applies to devices sold in the EU. However, most manufacturers are expected to adopt the same replaceable-battery design worldwide rather than maintain separate production lines. Readers outside Europe may benefit from the change even though the law does not apply to their region.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Does the Amazon Kindle currently have a replaceable battery?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>No. Current Kindle models have sealed, glued-in batteries. However, leaked firmware 5.19.4 reveals that Amazon is building battery replacement support into upcoming models. A next-generation Kindle with user-replaceable batteries is expected before the February 2027 deadline.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Does Kobo have replaceable batteries?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. Kobo has partnered with iFixit to sell genuine replacement batteries and repair kits for current models including the Clara BW, Clara Colour, Libra Colour, and Sage. Replacement batteries cost $20 to $70 depending on the model. Kobo is the only major e-reader brand with an established battery replacement program.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Will the EU Battery Law make e-readers more expensive?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Prices may rise moderately as manufacturers absorb the cost of redesigning chassis, sourcing new components, and setting up spare-parts supply chains. However, the long-term cost of ownership should decrease because users replace batteries instead of buying entirely new devices every 3-5 years.</p> </div> </div>
      `}/>
